<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>The most intriguing geometrical objects are symmetrical.  An 
      abstract group is an algebraic construct designed to quantify
      symmetry precisely.  And so, the study of groups and geometry 
      in tandem is both natural and symbiotic.  These notes are
      intended to introduce upper-level undergraduate students to
      their beautiful interplay.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-1">
    <title>Regular Polygons</title>
    <p>Let <m>P</m> be a regular polygon with <m>n</m> sides.  
      Thus, <m>P</m> consists of <m>n</m> points (vertices), 
      <m>p_0, \dots, p_{n-1}</m>X</m>, and <m>n</m> line segments,
      <m>p_0p_1, \dots, p_{n-1}p_0</m>.  Indices are to be read
      modulo <m>n</m>.  To be a <em>regular</em> polygon, we
      require that each side has the same length and that each
      angle 
      <m>\angle p_{k-1}p_kp_{k+1}</m> <m>(k = 0, \dots, n-1)</m>
      has the same measure.
    </p>
    <p>How many symmetries does <m>P</m> admit?</p>
    <p><em>Whenever a question is posed in these notes, it is 
      intended that the reader should pause and think.</em>
    </p>
    <p>The polygon has both rotational symmetry and mirror symmetry.
      We will now demonstrate one method of finding all of the 
      symmetries of <m>P</m>.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>A frog</caption>
      <image source="frog.jpg" width="50%">
        <description>
          <p>A picture of frog that hopped into a geometry paper.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>Question for later: why does the <m>\CAT</m> operator display in this font?</p>
  </subsection>
</section>
